\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Fits a straight line between two methods that are both measured with
error, using bivariate least squares (BLS) in the plot of one method
against the other and correlated bivariate least squares (CBLS) in the
Bland-Altman (mean-difference) plot (Francq & Govaerts, 2014, 2016). The
measurement error variances of the two methods are known, or estimated
from replicates, and may differ between the methods and between subjects.
}
\details{
\strong{Data.} Each subject \code{i} contributes the mean of its \code{n_xi} measurements
by \code{x} and its \code{n_yi} measurements by \code{y}. The variance of the measurement
error of these means is \code{var_x / n_xi} and \code{var_y / n_yi}, so subjects
with more replicates get more weight; with the same number of replicates
for every subject the variances are constant.

\strong{Error variances that change with the level.} When \code{var_x} or \code{var_y} is
a function, or \code{var_model} is \code{"proportional"} or \code{"power"}, the error
variance of each subject's measurements is evaluated at its estimated
true values (the points on the line nearest the data, given the error
variances), not at the observed means, whose own errors would bias the
weights. The fit starts with the variances at the observed means and is
repeated, re-evaluating them at the new estimated true values, until the
line is stable. With replicates, \code{"proportional"} estimates the
coefficient of variation from the within-subject variances relative to
the squared subject means (\code{sum(n_i - 1)} df); \code{"power"} regresses the log
within-subject variances (corrected for the bias of the log of a variance
estimate) on the log subject means, weighted by their df
(\code{sum(n_i - 1) - 1} df). For prediction intervals, the variances of the new
measurements are evaluated at the new value of \code{x} and the fitted \code{y} (or,
in the Bland-Altman plot, at the mean plus and minus half the fitted
difference); the uncertainty in an estimated \code{theta} is not included.

\strong{BLS.} The line \verb{y = b0 + b1 x} minimises
\verb{sum((y_i - b0 - b1 x_i)^2 / (var_yi + b1^2 var_xi))} and is found by the
iterative BLS algorithm (Lisy et al., 1990; Francq & Govaerts, 2014),
started from the Deming solution. With constant variances it is Deming
regression with \code{error.ratio = var_x / var_y}. Confidence intervals use
\code{t} on \code{n - 2} df with the standard errors below.

\strong{Standard errors.} BivRegBLS (\code{se_method = "bivregbls"}) uses
\verb{s^2 R^-1}, where \code{R} is the weighted cross-product matrix of \verb{(1, x_i)}
with weights \verb{1 / (var_yi + b1^2 var_xi)} and \code{s^2} the weighted residual
variance on \code{n - 2} df. This treats the observed \code{x_i} as if they were the
true values and the error variances as known, and is too small when the
error of \code{x} is not small relative to the spread of the true values (in
simulations, coverage of 95\% intervals fell to 0.87 with one measurement
each and to 0.71 with linked replicates). The default
(\code{se_method = "delta"}) has two parts. The first is the sandwich of the
estimating equations with \code{R} built from the estimated true values and
the error left in them removed, which with equal weights is the
errors-in-variables variance of Fuller (1987, sec. 1.3). The second, when
the error variances are estimated (from replicates, or given with finite
\code{df_var_x}, \code{df_var_y}), is a delta-method term for their uncertainty:
the derivatives of the coefficients with respect to the log error
variances (and, with linked replicates, the error correlation), taken
numerically over the whole fit, times their sampling covariance on their
df. With only \code{error.ratio}, the error variances are scaled by \code{s^2}. In
simulations the default covered 0.93 to 0.98 where BivRegBLS's covered
0.71 to 0.96, and the two agree when the true values are widely spread.
The uncertainty in the estimated equation error (\code{tau^2}) and in the
shape of a \code{"power"} variance function is not included.
\code{\link[=error_ratio_profile]{error_ratio_profile()}} uses the BivRegBLS standard errors.

\strong{CBLS.} In the Bland-Altman plot, with \code{D = x - y} and \code{M = (x + y) / 2}
(the convention of \code{\link[=agreement_limit]{agreement_limit()}}), the errors of \code{D} and \code{M} are
correlated, \code{cov = (var_x - var_y) / 2}. CBLS fits \verb{D = g0 + g1 M} with the
residual variance \verb{var_D + g1^2 var_M - 2 g1 cov}. It describes the same
line as BLS (Francq & Govaerts, 2016), with its own standard errors; the
bias is constant when \code{g1 = 0}.

\strong{Prediction intervals} (\code{predict(interval = "prediction")}) are for a
new measurement by \code{y} (or a new difference) given a new measurement by \code{x}
(or a new mean), each the mean of \code{reps_x}, \code{reps_y} replicates: the
variance is that of the line plus the measurement error variances, with
Satterthwaite degrees of freedom from \code{n - 2}, \code{df_var_x} and \code{df_var_y}.
For CBLS, the covariance between the errors of the new difference and the
new mean is included (BivRegBLS omits it; the two agree when
\code{var_x = var_y}).

\strong{Tolerance limits (experimental).} \code{predict(interval = "tolerance")}
gives tolerance limits around the line: with confidence \code{tol_level}, they
contain at least a proportion \code{pred_level} of new measurements by \code{y} at
the given \code{x} (\code{bound_type = "joint"}), or bound each prediction limit
(\code{"iu"}). New measurements are normal about the true line with the spread
\code{s^2} (their measurement error variances and any equation error), and the
fitted line is off by an unknown amount with its standard error. Without
equation error, \code{s^2} gets an upper confidence bound from the df of the
error variances (MOVER, as in \code{\link[=tolerance_limit]{tolerance_limit()}}; exact when they are
known) and the line a \code{t} bound on \code{n - 2} df; the joint limits are then
the \code{pred_level} quantile of the absolute value of a normal variable with
these bounds as mean and SD. With equation error, the residuals estimate
the spread of the subject means about the line on \code{n - 2} df, and that
part of \code{s^2} and the variance of the line share a chi-square bound;
with one measurement per subject the joint limits are then the textbook
regression tolerance limits (Wallis's approximation). The \code{"iu"} limits
bound each prediction limit with the MOVER. Francq and Govaerts (2016)
give tolerance intervals only for a constant bias (see
\code{\link[=tolerance_limit]{tolerance_limit()}}); these limits around a line are SimplyAgree's own
extension and are marked experimental. In simulations (95\% content and
confidence; 10 to 60 subjects), their confidence was 0.93 to 0.97 with
known error variances or with equation error and replicates, conservative
(up to 0.99) with replicates and no equation error, and 0.875 with 10
subjects, known variances, and equation error (study 4 in
\verb{references/general/cbls_sims/} of the package source).

\strong{Dispersion and equation error.} BLS assumes that the measurement errors
explain all the scatter about the line, i.e., that the true values lie on
it. Then the weighted residuals \verb{e_i^2 / (var_yi + b1^2 var_xi)} average 1;
their mean, the dispersion \code{s^2}, is reported by \code{summary()} with a test of
\code{s^2 = 1} (chi-square when the error variances are known, F against their
df when estimated). The confidence intervals of the coefficients are scaled
by \code{s^2} in any case. Item-specific deviations of the true values from the
line (equation error, e.g. method-by-item or matrix effects) make
\code{s^2 > 1}. They act like extra error variance that the replicates cannot
estimate, so ignoring them gives prediction intervals that are too narrow
and, through a wrong error ratio, a biased slope. With
\code{equation_error = TRUE}, each subject's true value is taken to deviate by
\code{c_xi} for \code{x} and \verb{b1 c_yi} for \code{y}, with \code{c ~ N(0, tau^2)} for both
methods (as \code{method_vc(tau = "common")}), so the error variances become
\code{var_xi + tau^2} and \verb{var_yi + b1^2 tau^2}. \code{tau^2} solves
\verb{sum(e_i^2 / (var_yi + b1^2 var_xi + 2 b1^2 tau^2)) = n - 2} (0 if the
dispersion is at most 1), and the fit is repeated until stable; the
prediction intervals include \code{tau^2} (on \code{n - 2} df). Equal deviations for
both methods is an assumption: with two methods, how the equation error
divides between them cannot be estimated.

\strong{Linked replicates.} When both methods measure each subject on the
same occasions and the true value varies between occasions (or the
conditions of an occasion affect both methods), the errors of \code{x} and \code{y}
about the subject's mean true value include a shared occasion effect, so
they are correlated. With \code{replicates = "linked"}, the within-subject
variances (which then include the occasion variation) are estimated from
the replicates as before, and the correlation \code{rho} of the two methods'
errors from the pooled within-subject cross-products of the paired
occasions (standardised by the error variances at the subject means, so
that it also applies with \code{var_model = "proportional"} or \code{"power"}), on
\code{sum(k_i - 1)} df, with \code{k_i} the subject's number of occasions with both
methods. The covariance of the errors of the subject means is
\verb{rho sqrt(var_x var_y) k_i / (n_xi n_yi)}, and it enters the BLS and CBLS
fits (\verb{var_y + b1^2 var_x - 2 b1 cov}), the dispersion, and the
prediction and tolerance intervals, which are for new measurements by
both methods on the same occasion(s). The measurement error part of these
intervals, a combination of the paired errors, is given the paired df.
The variances cannot be given (\code{var_x}, \code{var_y}): a measurement error
variance from a separate study does not include the occasion effects. In
simulations (15 or 40 subjects, 2 or 4 occasions), the prediction
intervals covered 0.95 to 0.97 (an exchangeable fit of the same data
covered up to 1.00, with intervals up to 2.9 times as wide), and the
confidence intervals of the coefficients 0.94 to 0.96 (0.91 to 0.95 with
\code{se_method = "bivregbls"} when the occasion effect was about three times
the larger measurement error SD).
This is the CBLS fit used by \code{\link[=method_convert]{method_convert()}} with
\code{replicates = "linked"} and \code{estimator = "cbls"}.

The joint test of intercept 0 and slope 1 (\code{\link[=joint_test]{joint_test()}}) uses the Wald
statistic with the BLS variance-covariance matrix and the \code{F(2, n - 2)}
distribution. BivRegBLS multiplies the critical value by the Hotelling
factor \code{(n - 1) / (n - 2)}, which is slightly more conservative.
}
\examples{
data("reps")
fit <- bls_reg(y ~ x, data = reps, id = "id")
fit
summary(fit)
predict(fit, newdata = c(4, 6), interval = "prediction")
plot(fit, interval = "prediction")
plot(fit, type = "md", interval = "prediction")
joint_test(fit)

# known error variances from a validation study, one measurement each
data("ba1986")
bls_reg(mini1 ~ wright1, data = ba1986, var_x = 400, var_y = 600)

# error SD proportional to the level (constant CV), known or estimated
bls_reg(mini1 ~ wright1, data = ba1986,
         var_x = function(level) (0.05 * level)^2,
         var_y = function(level) (0.07 * level)^2)
bls_reg(y ~ x, data = reps, id = "id", var_model = "proportional")

}
